cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software or study, please cite it using the metadata from this
  file and the Zenodo deposition.
title: >-
  Zar-Manah HELIOS v3.4: Where Remote CME Forecasting Stops — An Empirical
  Information-Ceiling Study Across Solar Cycles 23–25
abstract: >-
  An empirical study of the limits of remote CME arrival-time forecasting,
  built on a frozen catalogue of 43,559 CDAW CMEs, 610 matched CME–ICME
  associations and a held-out Solar Cycle 25 cohort. Two frozen transit models
  are evaluated on two frozen cohorts and all four cells are reported. On the
  full cohort (n = 126) the served and benchmark models are statistically
  indistinguishable (15.4713 h vs 15.4275 h; paired ΔMAE = +0.0438 h, 95 % CI
  [−0.8367, +0.9117], p = 0.94), while on the frozen one-to-one subset (n = 91)
  both improve by four to five hours (11.5792 h vs 10.5336 h). The cohort
  effect therefore dominates the model effect by a factor of five. Two design
  targets are reported as information-infeasible rather than redefined: a 90 %
  conformal interval narrower than 35 h (measured 74.0419 h at 0.9206 coverage;
  static floor 69.3126 h symmetric, 64.6250 h asymmetric) and an Apex Brier
  skill score above 0.25 for Dst ≤ −100 nT (measured 0.0768; oracle ceiling
  0.1500). The operational context is recomputed directly from the CCMC Earth
  CME Scoreboard public API and stated as a like-for-like caveat: the best
  public entry, 9.72 h, is obtained on 47 self-selected CMEs with human
  adjustment of the cone parameters, whereas HELIOS is fully automatic and runs
  on a single consumer CPU core.
